# One line. Run from the repo root by `orgasmic verify TASK-3NJ9K`.
#
# The natural red from the TASK-3NJ9K ask: with the fence reverted, a daemon
# test build resolves a real driver instead of failing. `injection.patch` puts
# `pair_refusal`/`transport_refusal` back to `None`, so `resolve_driver` hands
# out `acp-stdio/hermes` (and every other harness-exec pair) exactly as it did
# before this task, and the fence's own tests go red.
#
# `env -u ...` because a dispatch leaks its run id and home into subprocesses,
# and these tests must see neither.
#
# The dispatch-surface twin — `a_dispatch_naming_a_real_driver_is_refused_in_a_
# test_build`, which drives `spawn_worker_run` with a real driver — is
# deliberately NOT in this command. It is safe in the fixed tree only because
# the fence panics before `spawn_worker_run` builds a config or reaches
# `acquire`. Under the injection it would resolve `acp-stdio/hermes` and run on
# to acquire, which on any host with `hermes` on `$PATH` is a real provider
# subprocess and a billed turn: replaying it would reproduce the incident this
# task exists to prevent, not prove the fix. The fence tests below fail on the
# same reverted code without ever holding a driver session, so the red is
# pinned where it costs nothing.
env -u ORGASMIC_RUN_ID -u ORGASMIC_HOME cargo test -p orgasmic-daemon --lib driver_resolution::tests::
